ing to educate  the end user the consumer to avoid  and manage the food waste and as you can  see on the top of the slide  there are estimations on where the  food is wasted across the entire value  chain and we are trying to act  at every one of these different points  represented  at the top here uh to reduce uh  at least by 50 percent of food waste uh  in the next 10 years so it's an  ambitious program  but we're hoping to achieve at least  some significant  impact for society in that space  that i mentioned earlier uh at the  beginning of the presentation is the  state government grant called research  consortium program for agricultural  product development  so this is focusing on south australian  biomass  but we have multiple partners across the  country but  also international partners as well and  without going into  the details uh the program one is about  understanding the structure  of complex carbohydrates and bioactives  that we found in agricultural crops  program two is about assessing their  properties  um either biological activity and we're  talking for instance about anti-fungal  properties anti-inflammatory properties  antioxidant properties anti-cancer  properties  but also the structural properties of  carbohydrates like cellulose and starch  and caitin from mushrooms and program 3  is really about  developing products from these different  molecules from the waste  and scaling up the production of some of  the molecules  and also exploiting them commercially  eventually what we are trying to do is  to  use as close to 100 of the waste as  possible  so that we do not generate waste from  the waste  if i can say by doing sequential  extractions of the molecules  and minimizing the residue that we will  have at the end  there is no point uh targeting a  molecule that is  of super high value i can say but that  is present only at one to two percent in  the biomass  because if we do that we will still have  uh 95 98 percent of waste so we are  trying to  basically have an integrated sequential  process  that will extract a whole range of  compounds  sequentially and derive them into  products with a higher value  and again here adelaide glycomics is  playing a key role  in uh providing analytical support uh  through our partner laboratory with with  with agilent  to this different for the rest of my  presentation i decided to take some  examples  uh i could go on for quite a while but i  picked a few  examples to illustrate what we are doing  more concretely  so the first polymer we're interested in  is cellulose cellulose is present in  every single plant at different levels  depending on the type of crop  considerate but also the type of  issue so it varies from about 20 percent  to up to 80 percent depending  again on the type of tissues and  therefore it can be  used uh as a valuable product from  basically any type  of waste and this is what we had  first of all we can prepare cellulose in  different forms  in a cellulose nanofibrils abbreviated  by cnf here  cellulose nanocrystals abbreviated by  cnc  and i will provide examples uh uh in a  minute  about how to use cnf and cncs for  applications  um another form of cellulose that does  not come from plants is bacterial  cellulose  it is however extremely expensive to  produce  at the moment and it would be even if it  would become commercially  um viable it would still be for a niche  market  because it's also difficult to use on a  large scale  but the highest source of cellulose is  of course wood  the problem with wood cellulose is that  it's heavily lignified which means that  it needs to be  processed which is expensive and also  generates  a lot of solvent  issues that need to be managed but  cellulose as  summarized on this slide can be used for  many uh different applications  and i'm going to give you a little bit  more insight into that  next slides so if you would pull out  from your shirt your cotton shirt  a fiber uh it would look uh  like like it is represented at the top  of the slide  in other words it's a highly aggregated  network of cellulose microfibrils  and this network can be used obviously  for textile and paper  but we can do more useful things if we  dissociate this network  and there are two ways two main ways to  doing that  one is through mechanical shearing the  other one is by combining mechanical  shearing with enzymatic hydrolysis  and when you do that you basically  dissociate the sub elements  of this macro fibrils into  microfibrillated cellulose  and this microfibrillated cellulose can  be visualized with different forms of  microscopy  and it can be converted for instance in  hydrogels  that can be used for cosmetics  applications for instance  and other human applications so it's a  quite a straightforward  process if you  would make a nanopaper by simply drying  the microfiber related cellulose you can  derive  the cellulose in a transparent film that  is bendable  and that can also be modified  on the surface to make it for instance  water repellent  and design transparent self-cleaning  surfaces  the reason why this material is  transparent compared to normal  paper is because it consists of  microfibrillated cellulose and as you  can see  on this slide the pores of the nanopaper  compared to a classical  piece of paper are very small which  means that the light  does not diffuse as much as in a normal  paper  which creates opacity in the normal  paper  whereas the nano paper is instead  transparent  because it doesn't diffuse the light as  much as  this microfiber related cellulose can  also be  freeze dried to generate super tough  foams the material you see on this slide  consists only of 30  solid material the rest is air and it's  as light as polystyrene so it can be  used potentially for  packaging as an insulating material  because it's porous  but also as a carrier for pharmaceutical  and biomedical applications for instance  so it's super light  and super tough and it can be converted  into a whole range of  applications through a very simple  process again  dissociating the fibers and freeze  drying them  to generate another thing we can do  with cellulose is to hydrolyze it so  this is  how cellulose looks like if you extract  it from a primary cell wall from a plant  in a native form but if you treat it  with a strong acid  you will hydrolyze the least crystalline  parts of the cellulose microfibrils and  end up with what we call cellulose  whiskers  because they are similar in their  aspects as cat whiskers this is where  the name  comes from so these are basically uh the  most crystalline parts of the celosic  microfibrils  and they are quite homogeneous inside  they are in the range of 2 to 300  nanometers  so what can we do with this nano crystal  well we can suspend them uh in a liquid  and depending on the concentration of  the  nano crystals we can potentially  generate  or really generate i should say um what  is called a carol pneumatic phase  or if you like it's a liquid crystalline  phase and this is what is represented at  the bottom of the tube  if the sample is more diluted as  represented at the top of the tube  you end up with an isotropic suspension  where the nano crystals have a random  orientation  but as you concentrate the suspension  again represented at the bottom of the  tube a liquid crystalline phase  forms spontaneously so if we look at  what's happening in the bottom of the  bottom of the tube to the top you can  see that on each plane  the cellulose nano crystals which are  represented by these  small black lines slightly change  orientation by your small angle and as  you keep going up  along the tube the angle changes from  one plane to another  and this is a spontaneous process if you  will dry this material  you end up with the films that are  represented on the right hand side  that are irredesent in other words they  interfere with the transport of the  light through the material  or in other words the color changes the  color of the material changes depending  on the incidence of the light  on the material  by nature it's the same process as used  by  uh some insects uh there  there are no pigments in fact involved  here at all  it's just uh nanostructures in case of  the insects it's nanostructures made of  chitin  that provide iridescence so in this  particular case we have used cellulose  and if the nanocrystals are combined  with salt  we can make colored films  that again change color depending on the  incidence of light  examples of applications are in the  paints  for instance so you could have a paint  for your car made of this material  that changes color slightly depending on  the  incidence of the light from the sun on  the paint of your car and there are many  other potential  applications of the  in the other example i said at the  beginning we are quite strong in  carbohydrate and semology i will show  you a couple of examples  of what we can do with enzymes that act  on carbohydrate polymers  so here we have uh used an enzyme  that acts on alginates this is a  carbohydrates that is found in  seaweeds and it consists of manuronic  acid represented here in pink and  gularonic acid represented in blue  the enzyme we have isolated uh acts  uh faster on polymanuronic acid  stretches  um than it does on polyglonic acid  stretches so if you incubate  algae night from let's say brown seaweed  in the presence of this enzyme  it will release an unsaturated  monosaccharide  and this unsaturated monosaccharide is  spontaneously converted it's a  spontaneous reaction  into a molecule that we call deh for  deoxy  erythro exosulos uronic acid  and this molecule can be used as a  precursor for bioplastics but also  potentially  as a whitening agent for skin  formulations which has a lot of interest  in some parts of the world so  express this enzyme and characterize it  biochemically we have shown  that indeed it interacts more favorably  with stretches of manuronic acid than  guleronic acids  and we have determined its ph optimal ph  optimal temperature and it's actually  active  on a quite a large range of ph and  temperature which makes it very useful  potentially for industrial  so when we use the enzyme on  alginates it degrades preferentially  again the polymanuronic acid stretches  to generate dh  and this slide demonstrates that we are  able to produce dh  uh in a rigorously pure form and  the edge is actually which makes it uh  very easy to purify we have demonstrated  that the process can be scaled up this  is what is represented on this slide  so when we add the enzyme which is  abbreviated here by tc  arg1 to alginate in water  we can then simply incubate the enzyme  with the algae nite  it will release the dh molecule  which is soluble in ethanol so by simply  adding 95  ethanol we simply solubilize in one step  the deh molecule  and the rest remains insoluble  and the rest that is insoluble is the  part of the alginate  on which the enzyme has not acted and  this part of the alginate  is rich in guleronic acid remember the  enzyme doesn't act very well on urinic  acid  and this allows us to solubilize the dh  in ethanol  while retaining in an insoluble form a  gularonic acid-rich  alginate residue the dh can be used as i  said before as a precursor for  bioplastic but also  in skin whitening formulations  whereas guleronic acid as a polymer has  been shown to have  a biological activity anti-fungal  activity  and it is uh used at the moment in  clinical trials for the treatment of  respiratory diseases  against aspergillus so it has antifungal  properties but also other biological  activities  it can be used for the prevention of the  formation of biofilms for instance  a lot of potential applications the  system is fully scalable we have  produced here in a 50 milliliter  reaction  about 2 grams of ph and the rest  is alginate a third example  is about again the use of another enzyme  which belongs to the lytic  polysaccharide monooxygenase family  we have isolated this enzyme from a  fungal pathogen of rice visayan fuji  choroid  and we have expressed this enzyme  recombinantly  and purified it to homogeneity and this  is what this slide  illustrates here we have first expressed  it uh with the signal  sequence peptide uh that's the plb  sequence which allows the enzyme to be  secreted  in the culture medium and the  [Music]  signal peptide is removed by biopeptides  from from the bacterial strains  in which we have expressed it so once we  have purified  we were able to use it to modify chitin  for instance  so what this enzyme does is that it  breaks down  the chitin chain by creating  a carboxylic function at the reducing  ends  of the sugars that are modified and it  acts specifically  on carbon 1 on the of the sugar residues  what the enzyme does though is that it  does not completely degrade cartine  which is a big advantage here  because it can be used for surface  modification  only in other words we do not lose uh  the crystalline part of the chitin  so we can keep chitin fibers intact  while introducing carboxylic functions  at the surface of the chitin  which can then be used to encore  a whole range of molecules to  functionalize the surface of the chitin  in these examples we have used that you  can see in the box at the bottom of the  slide  fluorescein fitc fluorescein  isothiocyanate as  a modifying agent and also a small  peptide that consists of glycine  alanine and leucine as an example  of what can be done is represented here  so on the left hand side on panel a  you can see at the top the modification  of chitin which has become fluorescence  with fitc  and um that's panel a1  anal a2 is the same picture of the  commercial chitin  but in the bright field observation  under the optical microscope  finals three and four are just the  control without  the treatment with the enzyme so you can  see that the reaction is highly specific  on panel b we have  modified lobster shells directly with  the chitin or fragment of a lobster  shell  so panel one is the control and two and  three  are the fluorescently modified lobster  shells which are rich in chitin so the  enzymes  working even on solid materials so this  is  heterogeneous catalysis if you like it's  a very efficient  enzyme on the right hand side the mass  spectra  reflects also the capacity to modify  oligosaccharides with the same reaction  and modify them with peptides and this  can be used to make bioactive  oligosaccharides functionalized with  peptides for instance so the reaction is  very versatile we use a whole range of  molecules and this is summarized here  on this slide where again chitin is  first functionalized  with the enzyme the lpmo  to introduce carboxylic functions at the  surface  of the material followed then by the  grafting of a whole range of  compounds for functionalization and you  can see here a number of  specific applications  in this presentation which is about the  generation of uv protective materials  what we have done here is to exploit  small molecules called microsporines and  microsparine like amino acids  these molecules absorb uv-a  and uvb radiations and they are normally  produced by  different organisms algae in particular  but also some  cyanobacteria and the fish  that thrive in tropical waters  like the great barrier reef in in in  australia for instance  acquire these microsporines by eating  algae  and it's a way for them to protect  themselves against the  negative impact of the uv lights so what  we have done  in in this was to exploit these  microsporines to generate uv protective  materials so there are two main types of  microsporines  which are derivatives of cyclohexamine  and cyclohexenone  and they're basically amino acid  derivatives  of these core structures what is  important on this slide is the values of  the epsilon that you have at the bottom  these values reflect the molar  absorption coefficient of these  molecules  and it shows how powerful they are in  absorbing  uh ultraviolet ray radiations these  values are twice as high as what you  need  to claim that you have an efficient  sunscreen  product to be put on the market so  extremely  high capacity to absorb uv radiation so  we have simply  to make a conjugate uh with chitosan so  the molecules have carboxylic functions  and chitosan is a they are saturated  form of  chitin which carries three amino groups  so by a simple  amidation reaction we can produce  a stable conjugate a covalent conjugate  that combines the properties of the  chitosan with the uv absorbing  properties  of the microsporin  and the conjugate can be turned into a  very thin film  of about 30 micrometer thick the film is  completely transparent  and it blocks uh completely uva and uvb  radiation so unique properties here are  from this film  uh what is represented here is a  modification of the kitazan  by only a three percent of micros  increase uh the concentration to ten  percent of microsporines  uh you can see that the uv radiations  are completely blocked  whereas the transmittance in the visible  light  remains close to 100 in other words the  films remain completely transparent  while blocking completely uva and uvb  radiations  stable so here we have irradiated the  films  to an amount of uv  radiations that is equivalent to the  number of sun  to the amount of sun you get over a  period of nearly 10 years in queensland  and 15 years in victoria  and south australia and what this shows  is that  the film is stable under these high  uv radiation conditions in other words  it could be used as a coating material  for instance for outdoor furniture  and still be stable after 10 years in  queensland  and 15 years in south australia  and victoria so lots of potential  thermal resistance so if you cook it at  uh  close to 100 degrees uh for up to 12  hours  its absorption capacity does not change  and this applies to the different  microsporines we have tested so the  m glee uh the shinrin and the p334  are all stable after a conjugation to  kitozen  the control abbreviated here by cs uh  is the kaitozan itself that has not been  modified so you can see that the  absorption spectra  of the three conjugates we have made  with the three microsporines  uh are thermal resistant  biocompatible here we have grown skin  cells on the films and we have used the  live dead assay  to check the viability of the cells  in what happens here is that the cells  turn  red when they are dead and  in fact when they are alive they they  turn green  when the cells are alive they are able  to metabolize the fluorescent dye  that we have used which is normally red  and convert it into a  green fluorescent uh dye  if the cells are dead they are not able  to metabolize the dye  which remains red and therefore the red  the cells that are dead  are visible in in red color in other  words and in summary here you can see  that  we have basically no cell death  which means that the cells can grow  happily on this type of material which  shows their biocompatibility  and direct potential application for  in a human tissues so a lot of  versatility  shows that the cells stick very well to  the film  on the left it's the kaitozan that has  not been modified  on the right is the kaitos are modified  with the  glycine microsporine and you can see  that the cells  spread very well on the material so they  are highly biocompatible and they can be  used for  skin care products and protect the skin  against the impact of uv radiation so  this has been now  patented and we are developing it as a  commercial product  not only for skin care products but  also for coating materials outdoor  furniture  uh glasses outdoor textiles and  and many more materials this is the end  of my  presentation i hope you have enjoyed the  types of applications we can generate  these are only a few  examples i could continue for  quite a while and i will be happy  then to take questions and here i'm also  thanking of course the people that have  contributed to the work  especially from my lab in sweden  uh the names of the contributors are  highlighted  in green and thank you for your  attention and i hope you enjoyed this  presentation  thank you dr balloon for that  outstanding presentation  and we will now move to the live q a  portion of the presentation  as a reminder please submit your  questions via the q and a  box  so we'll give them a couple seconds to  get their questions in  would you like to add any comments to  our audience right now dr balloon or  waiting for some questions  yeah a question that i'm very often  asked you know this  is going to uh to come is uh how far you  know we are from commercialization  um it depends on the products i would  say  um in most instances we still  need to upscale the production of some  of these so for instance for the uv  protective materials  uh we are dependent on obtaining  microsporines on a larger scale  a way to do that is through a metabolic  engineering so reconstructing the  pathway  biosynthetic pathways in organisms we  can grow easily like  bacteria or yeast to produce the  molecules in large amounts but basically  we have made formulations that  work for skin care products for instance  other compounds that are dependent on  the use of enzymes  require again large scale production of  enzymes in fermenters  which can be done of course these  enzymes are easy to express we have  demonstrated that  for the for the nanocellulose it's  already a commercial product  and it can be used in very many  different applications there's a  manual growth rate compound annual  growth rate  predicted to be of around 30 percent for  the next  uh five to ten years for nanocellulose  and most of it is done  uh it's already produced industrially  and most of it comes from  from wood and it can go you know i gave  these examples here but it can go also  in composite materials to make um  reinforced materials for car panels or  even for a aeroplane panels  making materials super light but strong  at the same time  and so some of these products could go  on the market you know in the near  future they're all at different stages  and the limitations are always you know  upscaling  and also of course looking at the market  demand  and the cost of production  dr balloon i want to thank you for your  presentation today and your important  research  and i want to thank our audience members  and remind them that there are still  opportunities to ask questions questions  that were submitted but not answered  today by our speaker will be addressed  via email  and this presentation will be available  on demand viewing through september  2021 please share it with your colleague  who might have been  interested in this topic thank you again  for your participation  and i hope you will enjoy and join me  for the next presenter  georgia gonzalez thank you again dr  balloon  and if you're not thank you virtual  studio take care  be safe until next time everyone bye-bye  thank you bye-bye
 

